## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

---

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props or state change. For primitives, change means a different value. For objects and functions, change means a different **reference** — even if the contents are identical.

```js
// New object created every render → child always re-renders
<Child config={{ theme: 'dark' }} />

// New function created every render → child always re-renders
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

JavaScript's `===` compares objects by reference, not by content. Every render of the parent creates a brand-new object/function literal, so the prop always looks "changed" to React.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component to skip re-renders if its props haven't changed (shallow equality). Useless without stable prop references.

```js
const Child = React.memo(({ onClick, config }) => { ... });
```

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a function reference across renders. Use when passing callbacks as props to memoized children.

```js
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(id), [id]);
```

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a computed value. Use for expensive calculations, or to stabilize an object/array reference passed as a prop.

```js
const config = useMemo(() => ({ theme: 'dark', size: 'lg' }), []);
```

**When NOT to use them:** Don't wrap everything reflexively. Memoization has its own cost (memory, cache invalidation). Only apply when you've identified a real render problem or the computation is genuinely expensive.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

The most common context mistake: the value prop is an inline object.

```js
// Every render of App creates a new value object → all consumers re-render
<ThemeContext.Provider value={{ theme, setTheme }}>
```

Every component calling `useContext(ThemeContext)` re-renders whenever `App` renders, even if `theme` didn't change.

**Fix:** Memoize the context value, or split context into separate providers (read vs. dispatch).

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before — broken**

```jsx
function ProductList({ categoryId }) {
  const [items, setItems] = useState([]);

  // New function reference every render
  const handleSelect = (id) => console.log('selected', id);

  // New object reference every render
  const style = { padding: 16, margin: 8 };

  return items.map(item =>
    <ProductCard
      key={item.id}
      item={item}
      onSelect={handleSelect}  // Child re-renders every time ProductList renders
      style={style}
    />
  );
}

// memo does nothing — props change every render anyway
const ProductCard = React.memo(({ item, onSelect, style }) => (
  <div style={style} onClick={() => onSelect(item.id)}>
    {item.name}
  </div>
));
```

**After — fixed**

```jsx
// Stable outside the component — no memoization needed
const cardStyle = { padding: 16, margin: 8 };

function ProductList({ categoryId }) {
  const [items, setItems] = useState([]);

  // Stable reference across renders; only changes if categoryId changes
  const handleSelect = useCallback((id) => {
    console.log('selected', id);
  }, []); // no deps → truly stable

  return items.map(item =>
    <ProductCard
      key={item.id}
      item={item}
      onSelect={handleSelect}  // same reference → memo works
      style={cardStyle}        // same reference → memo works
    />
  );
}

const ProductCard = React.memo(({ item, onSelect, style }) => (
  <div style={style} onClick={() => onSelect(item.id)}>
    {item.name}
  </div>
));
```

---

### Summary

| Cause | Fix |
|---|---|
| Inline object/array prop | `useMemo` or lift outside component |
| Inline function prop | `useCallback` |
| Child renders on every parent render | `React.memo` (requires stable props) |
| Context consumers over-render | Memoize context value; split read/write contexts |

The right order: **profile first** (React DevTools Profiler), then fix the specific bottleneck. Premature memoization adds noise without benefit.
